-- title:
--   Cubes of the tiling and the maximum norm on $\mathbb{R}^d$
-- statement:
--   Work in $\mathbb{R}^d$. The module introduces the maximum norm
--
--   $$\|x\|_{\infty} \;=\; \sup_{i} |x_i|$$
--
--   (a supremum over the finitely many coordinates $i \in \{1,\dots,d\}$), together with three families of cubes of side length $h$ centred at a point $u$:
--
--   $$A_h(u) = \{x : \|x-u\|_\infty < h/2\}, \qquad \tilde A_h(u) = \prod_{i}\big[u_i - h/2,\ u_i + h/2\big), \qquad \bar A_h(u) = \{x : \|x-u\|_\infty \le h/2\},$$
--
--   the open cube, the half-closed cube, and the closed cube. It also records the extensionality principle that two points of $\mathbb{R}^d$ agreeing in every coordinate are equal — needed because the Euclidean space is a type synonym rather than a plain function type.
--
--   The three variants are not interchangeable and are all kept deliberately: the half-closed cubes are the ones that *tile* $\mathbb{R}^d$, so every point lies in exactly one of them, which is what makes a lattice-indexed piecewise-constant approximation well defined; the open cube is the one whose Lebesgue measure appears in averaging statements; and the closed cube is the one the renormalisation arguments use, where a non-strict inequality is wanted.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The file comment flags that the source uses a strict inequality in its definition of $A_h(u)$ but a non-strict one when the notation is recalled later, so the two are genuinely different sets and both are carried. No positivity hypothesis is imposed on $h$: for $h \le 0$ the open and half-closed cubes are empty and the closed cube degenerates, so the intended reading requires $h > 0$ at the use sites.
